{% call note("care", "Take care", "Keep access in your control") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep passwords and recovery codes in your control. Do not send them in an enquiry form or attach them to this checklist. If someone else will use the computer, record that as a scope question rather than assuming every profile is included.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="section-2"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Check the starting condition</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Can the old computer start normally and read the files you want to move? Is there a usable backup? Is the new device the platform you expected? If the answer is uncertain, explain that before booking.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Windows Backup is one Microsoft option to understand, but it is not a promise that every file, application or setting will be recreated. Review <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/backup-recovery/back-up-and-restore-with-windows-backup">Microsoft's backup and restore guidance</a> against your circumstances. Do not assume a visible cloud icon proves every important item is protected.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Do not start a transfer or erase anything") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">This preparation guide does not ask you to start a transfer or erase anything. If the source will not start, storage is inaccessible or a recovery prompt is unfamiliar, pause for assessment.</p>{% endcall %}
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If your backup plan is unclear, start with <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/home-computer-backup/">setting up a home-computer backup you can check</a> before changing the old computer.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="section-3"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Agree what “finished” means</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Choose a few representative files and everyday tasks from your essentials list. These become handover checks, not a guarantee that sampling proves every byte transferred. Keep a list of exceptions or work that will continue afterwards.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Our <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/new-computer-handover-checks/">post-move checklist</a> helps you prepare those questions. Keep the old computer and original files until you understand the result; disposal is a separate decision.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If you have not yet decided whether to replace the old computer, start with <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/slow-computer-fix-upgrade-replace/">fix, upgrade or replace</a>.</p>

